# .gitignore for db1's git-init'd /srv/ (repo root is /srv/, not /srv/docker/
# -- other /srv siblings exist: cron/, docker-replicated/). Host-specific --
# see gitignore-db2 for db2's separate copy; do not merge the two, since a
# finding specific to one host (a stale duplicate directory, a data-path
# only that host has) can wrongly exclude something the other host actually
# needs tracked, or leave a no-op pattern for something that never existed
# there in the first place. `git init` runs from /srv/ itself, not
# /srv/docker/.
#
# ---- scope: only docker/ is tracked, everything else at /srv root is ignored ----
# cron/, docker-replicated/, glusterfs/ (and anything else that shows up at the
# /srv root later) are out of scope for this repo entirely -- not audited, not
# reviewed for secrets, just excluded outright. Revisit deliberately if this
# repo's scope is ever meant to grow beyond docker/.
/*
!/docker/
!/.gitignore
!/.githooks/

# ---- everything below only ever applies inside docker/, since that's the only
#      top-level entry not excluded above ----
#
# This file holds only host-wide GENERIC rules (secret file patterns, runtime
# data, stale directories, nested repos). It deliberately does NOT list any
# specific per-service file (e.g. "gitlab-runner/config.toml") -- those live
# in a per-directory .gitignore instead (e.g. /srv/docker/gitlab-runner/.gitignore
# containing just "config.toml"), one level down, right next to the file it
# excludes. That's because db1 and db2 can have same-named service
# directories (watchtower, restic, patroni, gitlab-runner, ...) whose actual
# content differs -- a file secret-bearing on one host may already be clean
# on the other. A per-directory .gitignore is self-contained and host-specific
# by construction; a shared root-level list of specific paths is not, and
# copying one host's findings into the other's repo would wrongly exclude a
# file that host never needed excluded.
#
# sanitize_service_files.py --write generates each service's .gitignore for
# you automatically (only for services where it actually found something to
# redact) -- review its output and copy it to
# /srv/docker/<service>/.gitignore. This root file only needs manual edits
# for genuinely host-wide patterns or genuinely root-level (/docker/<file>)
# loose files.

# ---- secrets: never track ----
.env
.env.*
# .env.example is the deliberate exception: it's the git-trackable companion
# to .env (secrets blanked, config values kept as real values -- built by
# build_env_example.py from a service's real .env). ".env.*" above would
# otherwise silently exclude it too, since it also starts with ".env." --
# found only once we actually tried to track one for the first time, meaning
# every .env.example anywhere in this repo had been silently excluded the
# entire time before this fix.
!.env.example
*.env
*.env_*
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*.p12
*.der
htpasswd
users.acl
ldap.toml

# NOTE: .gitignore does NOT support trailing inline comments -- a "#"
#      after a pattern on the same line is parsed as part of the pattern
#      itself (making it match nothing), not stripped as a comment. Comments
#      must be on their own line, before the pattern.
#
# /srv/docker/config.toml on db1 is currently an EMPTY directory (checked
# directly) -- nothing to exclude there right now. Not the same finding as
# db2's config.toml (a real file with a runner-registration token in it) --
# see gitignore-db2 for that one. Revisit if this ever becomes a real file.

# ---- loose top-level files at /srv/docker root, deferred for now (not yet
#      reviewed for secrets -- run_all.sh only iterates service directories,
#      so these were never scanned). /docker/minio.tar.gz is already caught
#      by the *.tar.gz pattern below; these two aren't covered by anything
#      else. Revisit deliberately later; excluded here in the meantime so
#      they don't get swept into a commit unreviewed. ----
/docker/update_watchtower_images.py
/docker/watchtower_updates.txt

# ---- runtime data / logs / backups: not config, don't track ----
*.log
*.rdb
data/
restored-data/
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tar.bz2

# ---- editor/OS artifacts: never intentional, safe to exclude anywhere ----
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store

# ---- a log file that doesn't match *.log above because of its actual
#      extension -- found via `find -size +50M`: 91M of ldap/'s 92M total is
#      this one file. Anchored since "logs.txt" isn't a name worth banning
#      globally (too generic, could collide with something legitimate
#      elsewhere). ----
/docker/ldap/logs.txt

# ---- large, non-standard-named data directories found by actual `du` review
#      (db1) -- same reasoning as data/ above, just not named "data" so the
#      generic pattern doesn't reach them. Anchored to the exact path: these
#      are genuinely huge (105G, 2.5G, 7G) runtime data/cache/backup dirs
#      sitting alongside otherwise-small config in the same service
#      directory, not a reason to exclude the whole service. ----
/docker/devpi/devpi-data/
/docker/devpi/.indices/
/docker/gitlab-ee/metrics/
/docker/xpressfeed/backup/
/docker/xpressfeed/Desktop/

# ---- vendored third-party binaries bundled into a service's runtime data
#      dir, found via `git ls-files` size review -- not config, not secrets,
#      just re-downloadable application dependencies that would otherwise
#      sit in git history forever, uncompressible and undiffable. ----
# Grafana's own plugin/runtime data dir (30-40MB binary datasource plugins);
# grafana-loki's actual hand-authored config (datasources/, dashboards/)
# lives in separate sibling directories and stays tracked normally.
/docker/grafana-loki/grafana/
# etcd release tarball extracted directly into patroni/ instead of using a
# container image (etcd + etcdutl binaries, ~36M).
/docker/patroni/etcd-v3.5.9-linux-amd64/

# ---- nested repos: managed by their own .git, not this one ----
# Found via a full `find /srv/docker -name .git` sweep, not just the
# nested-git dirs that were already obvious by name -- dockprom/,
# netbox-docker/, elastdocker/ (customized clones of upstream projects) are
# the ones already known; shhh/, docker-rclone-sync/, samba-ldap/ are entire
# service directories that turned out to ALSO be their own git clones.
# Excluded wholesale here so the top-level repo doesn't double-track their
# files under two different repos' history. Commit their local changes
# inside each directory itself: `cd docker/dockprom && git add/commit`
# (same for the others). db1 has no zulip/ -- that's db2's nested repo, see
# gitignore-db2.
dockprom/
netbox-docker/
elastdocker/
shhh/
docker-rclone-sync/
samba-ldap/

# ---- nested repos living in a SUBDIRECTORY of an otherwise-normal service --
# same reasoning as above (own .git, own history, don't double-track), but
# narrow enough that the rest of the service's config stays trackable:
# uptime-kuma/'s actual config is fine, just this one report-generator clone
# inside it isn't; same for ldap/'s webui and devpi/'s stray nested clone
# (this is what the earlier "docker-devpi/" 288K oddity turned out to be). ----
/docker/uptime-kuma/Uptime-Kuma-Report/
/docker/ldap/ldap-passwd-webui/
/docker/devpi/docker-devpi/

# ---- live, dedicated secrets-management / HA-cluster services: deliberately
#      excluded from the bulk/scripted pass entirely (see run_all.sh's SKIP
#      array) and handled by hand, as root, when actually working on them --
#      but SKIP only controls the sanitizer, not git, so these need an
#      explicit exclusion here too or `git add -A` would stage their raw,
#      never-scanned contents. Shared across hosts; a no-op where the
#      directory doesn't exist. ----
vault/
vault2/
infisical/
pg-cluster-instance-1/
pg-cluster-instance-2/
healthchecks/
nessie/
sentry/

# ---- stale/duplicate/abandoned directories worth deleting outright, ignored here as a fallback ----
# (grafana-loki-old/, sentry-old/, sentrytest/, infisical_test/, http-test/,
#  gitlab-ee-failed/, *.back.yaml, *.bak, *.new.yaml -- all caught generically
#  below; better to `rm` these than carry them forever)
# NOTE: gitignore patterns are case-sensitive on Linux -- "*.bak" alone missed a
# real "CA.pem.BAK" file during review (on db2). Bracket expressions below match either case.
*.[bB][aA][kK]
*-old/
*_old/
*test/
*failed/
*.back.yaml
*.new.yaml

# ---- cert/key material directories: never track ----
# e.g. pg-cluster-instance-*/certs/, certs_old/, pki/, redis-staging/tmp-conf/
certs/
certs_old/
pki/
tmp-conf/

# db2 had a known junk-file case (literally named ".crt"/".csr", empty
# basename, leftover from a botched command) excluded by exact anchored path
# -- not yet checked whether db1's proxy/ has the same issue. Worth a look:
#   ls -la /srv/docker/proxy/ | grep -E '^\.(crt|csr)$'
# See gitignore-db2 for that exclusion if db1 turns out to have the same thing.

# ---- everything else inside docker/ ----
# Any file not covered by a rule above (a clean docker-compose.yaml, nginx.conf,
# healthcheck.py, redis.conf that had nothing to redact, etc.) is tracked as
# its real, unmodified self by default. Per-service .gitignore files (one per
# service directory, generated by sanitize_service_files.py --write when it
# finds something, or added by hand for a known case like
# gitlab-runner/config.toml) are what carve out the specific exceptions.
